WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.307
IS DISTINCT FROM is one of PostgreSQL's most useful NULL-safe comparison tools.

00:00:05.307 --> 00:00:10.667
Ordinary equality and inequality can produce UNKNOWN when NULL is involved.

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:15.337
IS DISTINCT FROM always gives a definite true-or-false answer.

00:00:15.337 --> 00:00:19.242
If two known values are equal, they are not distinct.

00:00:19.242 --> 00:00:22.862
If two known values are different, they are distinct.

00:00:22.862 --> 00:00:28.572
If both values are NULL, PostgreSQL treats them as not distinct for this comparison.

00:00:28.572 --> 00:00:32.862
If one is NULL and the other is a known value, they are distinct.

00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:38.092
There is also IS NOT DISTINCT FROM, which behaves like NULL-safe equality.

00:00:38.092 --> 00:00:44.742
Suppose you are comparing yesterday's customer profile with today's profile to identify changes.

00:00:44.742 --> 00:00:50.347
If both phone numbers are NULL, you probably do not want to mark that as a change.

00:00:50.347 --> 00:00:56.037
Ordinary comparisons make that awkward because NULL introduces UNKNOWN.

00:00:56.037 --> 00:01:01.757
IS DISTINCT FROM lets you say: tell me whether these two fields are meaningfully different,

00:01:01.757 --> 00:01:07.097
including their missingness. Another example is a payment status rule.

00:01:07.097 --> 00:01:13.737
If you want everything that is not definitely Paid, a missing status should count as different from Paid.

00:01:13.737 --> 00:01:17.252
IS DISTINCT FROM expresses that clearly.

00:01:17.252 --> 00:01:23.422
Use this tool when NULL is part of the comparison itself and you want a definite Boolean result instead of

00:01:23.422 --> 00:01:24.547
UNKNOWN.
